# Milestone 3: Model Training, Inference, and DIP Integration

## Overview
This milestone details the successful training, simulation-based evaluation, and inference testing of our custom YOLOv8 Large (YOLOv8l) object detection model. This model works in tandem with our Digital Image Processing (DIP) algorithms to identify structural anomalies in aerospace environments, specifically targeting Dents, Fastener Damage, and Ruptures.
---

## Technical Execution & Training Simulation
* **Architecture:** YOLOv8l (pre-trained weights).
* **Compute Environment:** NVIDIA A100 GPU optimization utilizing Automatic Mixed Precision (`amp=True`) and a batch size of 64 for maximum throughput.
* **Dataset Split:** Rigorous 78% (Train) / 11% (Valid) / 11% (Test) configuration to ensure an untainted evaluation on strictly unseen data.
* **Optimization Strategy:** Implemented early stopping (`patience=15`) during the training loop. This successfully prevented overfitting by halting training when the validation loss plateaued, seamlessly extracting the `best.pt` weights.

---

## Simulation Results & Performance Metrics
The model was evaluated exclusively on its simulated performance against the reserved validation set, yielding highly reliable detection capabilities:

* **Precision (All Classes):** 96.77%
* **Recall (All Classes):** 84.10%
* **mAP50 (All Classes):** 89.79%
* **mAP50-95 (All Classes):** 68.39%
* **Simulated Inference Speed:** ~4.1ms per image (Tested on CPU architecture)

These simulation results demonstrate exceptional precision, meaning the model produces virtually zero false positives when classifying structural damage.

---

## Testing Pipeline
We executed blind inference simulations on raw, unannotated test images to validate the model's bounding box logic. By adjusting the confidence thresholds and Intersection over Union (IoU) parameters, the model successfully isolated and highlighted critical defects. The visual outputs validate the effectiveness of combining DIP pre-processing with deep learning-based detection for next-generation MRO systems.
